and all-control outcomes from exposure features, then difference) recover the truth, and which features of the interference mechanism, graph and assignment design determine when it works and when it fails?\",\n  \"central_thesis\": \"On an eight-case synthetic grid (n=3000; SBM, Barabasi-Albert, Watts-Strogatz graphs; additive, strong, saturating, degree-dependent and two-hop interference; Bernoulli and cluster assignment), regression-based GATE error is small (about 3-5% relative RMSE per case) when the fitted exposure mapping matches the generating mechanism, is large and largely systematic when it does not (saturating and two-hop cases), and the nominal 95% intervals undercover (0.839 mean coverage), so accuracy is governed by exposure-mapping fidelity rather than by graph family, interference strength or assignment design within the tested range; a BIC-gated parametric exposure library lowers the sealed-final mean relative RMSE to 0.126 versus 0.180 for the plain linear exposure regression, but does not fix the two-hop case.\",\n  \"narrative_rationale\": \"The sealed final evaluation is completed with outcome 'qualified' (final.qualification.outcome), 24 unseen seeds x 8 cases, no missing or failed episodes, and the four evaluator-owned baselines (difference-in-means, linear exposure regression, neighbourhood Horvitz-Thompson, correctly specified oracle regression) plus negative controls were run on the same instances. That supports a calibrated empirical paper on the research question posed in the task: how accurate, what determines the error, where it fails. The strongest grounded story is not 'a new estimator wins' but a thesis-first characterization: the per-case final errors (0.031-0.054 under additive/strong/cluster/graph-family variations, 0.151 degree-dependent count, 0.198 saturating, 0.452 two-hop) show that error tracks exposure-mapping misspecification, with the oracle (0.045) as the correct-specification reference and the linear baseline (0.180), DiM (0.622) and HT (0.994) as the cost of ignoring or non-parametrically using exposure. The frozen estimator is a vehicle to measure this, not the headline. The honest negative results (undercovering intervals, unresolved two-hop case, flexible ensemble and residual-correction variants failing to beat linear on the search plane) are retained as findings. Qualification is a measured-result statement only; it is not evidence of generality or validity beyond the eight declared cases.\",\n  \"prior_paper_story_status\": \"rejected\",\n  \"edit_framing_decision\": \"Not in edit mode: no previous_paper or staged programs exist. A fresh framing was produced. Candidate framings compared: (a) 'new BIC-gated estimator beats baselines' (rejected: selection on search plane used the same case specifications as the final plane, only 3 scored model variants, no ablation of the gate, per-case baseline numbers unavailable, so a method-superiority headline would be over-claimed); (b) 'search/iteration story' (rejected: chronology); (c) thesis-first characterization of regression-GATE accuracy and its determinants with the frozen estimator as the studied method (chosen).\",\n  \"staged_input_impact\": [\n    \"No staged prior manuscript or staged new programs; no downstream_study block exists in paper_data.json, so no downstream-study claims may be made. Framing rests on final_evaluation (sealed final, completed, qualified) plus search-plane variant results kept strictly separate.\"\n  ],\n  \"method_groups\": [\n    {\n      \"name\": \"Frozen BIC-gated parametric exposure-regression GATE estimator (studied estimator)\",\n      \"role\": \"proposed\",\n      \"include_reason\": \"It is the single frozen selection evaluated on the sealed final plane; library of OLS-fitted exposure mappings (linear neighbour fraction, treated-neighbour count, two-hop fraction, saturating/sigmoid shapes, degree and covariate interactions), analytic all-treated/all-control feature values, BIC-margin gate (margin 10) with linear fallback, HC1 delta-method interval widened by the departure from linear. Describe only what is in the program source; the gate's per-case behaviour is unknown (diagnostics unverified).\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"name\": \"Plain linear exposure regression (own treatment, neighbour-treated fraction, covariates)\",\n      \"role\": \"baseline\",\n      \"include_reason\": \"Canonical regression-GATE estimator (Chin-style); final mean relative RMSE 0.180; the reference against which the frozen estimator's ratio 0.701 is reported.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"name\": \"Difference-in-means and neighbourhood-exposure Horvitz-Thompson\",\n      \"role\": \"baseline\",\n      \"include_reason\": \"Interference-ignorant and design-based comparators (final 0.622 and 0.994); show why modelling exposure matters and that HT is high-variance at n=3000.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"name\": \"Oracle correctly specified exposure regression\",\n      \"role\": \"supporting\",\n      \"include_reason\": \"Evaluator-owned reference (final 0.045) using the true mechanism form; marks the floor achievable by regression when specification is right, separating misspecification error from sampling error.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"name\": \"Estimator-class alternatives on the search plane (flexible ridge ensemble with extrapolation-aware averaging; linear plus design-weighted residual correction)\",\n      \"role\": \"ablation\",\n      \"include_reason\": \"Search-plane (8 seeds) scores 0.232 and 0.185 vs linear 0.184 show that added flexibility or residual correction did not help; presented as a clearly labelled search-plane comparison of estimator classes, never as final evidence and never as a chronology.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"name\": \"Negative controls (constant outcome, permuted outcomes, permuted/relabelled graph) and deterministic replay\",\n      \"role\": \"supporting\",\n      \"include_reason\": \"Validity checks on the frozen estimator: constant-outcome max |estimate| 9.0e-15, permuted-outcome mean relative estimate 0.0588, permuted-graph RMSE 0.627, 0 vacuity violations, replay difference 0, 0 missing/failed episodes.\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"results_focus\": [\n    \"Headline accuracy view (final plane, 24 unseen seeds x 8 cases, n=3000): mean relative RMSE of the frozen estimator 0.126 vs oracle 0.045, linear 0.180, DiM 0.622, HT 0.994 (ratios 0.701 to linear, 0.203 to DiM). Relative error is min(|est-GATE|/|GATE|,2), RMSE per case then mean over cases; state this metric and why (scale-free across mechanisms with different GATE sizes, cap prevents HT blow-ups dominating).\",\n    \"Per-case error profile and single-factor contrasts (final.metric.rmse_rel_case_1..8): 1 SBM additive Bernoulli 0.051; 2 cluster design 0.031; 3 BA 0.054; 4 WS 0.040; 5 strong interference 0.034; 6 saturating sigmoid 0.198; 7 BA count x degree-dependent 0.151; 8 two-hop 0.452. Contrasts against case 1 (design, graph family, strength, response shape, spillover range) and case 3 vs 7 (mechanism form on BA). Within these cases graph family, interference strength and cluster vs Bernoulli assignment do not degrade accuracy; response-shape and spillover-range misspecification do. State that each contrast is a single case pair with 24 seeds and no per-case interval is available; hypothesis support is 'consistent with', not tested by factorial design.\",\n    \"Bias versus variance: mean absolute relative bias 0.074 against mean RMSE 0.126, i.e. a large systematic component aggregated over cases; per-case bias is not in the artifacts, so do not assign bias/variance split per case.\",\n    \"Uncertainty quantification of the intervals: coverage 0.839 against nominal 0.95 (mean over cases), mean relative width 0.379; report as undercoverage, a negative result. Per-case coverage is unavailable.\",\n    \"Seed-level variability: per-seed evaluator scores over the 24 final episodes (range about 0.078 to 0.180, two clear outliers at seeds 9001 and 9015); report mean, spread and a bootstrap interval computed from final.evidence.episodes, flagged as seed-level (plane-score) variability, not per-case.\",\n    \"Estimator-class comparison on the search plane only (8 seeds, same 8 case specifications, different seeds): flexible ridge ensemble 0.232, linear+residual correction 0.185, BIC-gated parametric library 0.140 vs linear 0.184 on that plane; per-case table shows that the gain concentrates in case 6 (0.708 to 0.267) while case 8 stays near 0.445. Selection of the frozen estimator happened on this plane; keep search and final separate and say final-plane numbers are the evidence.\",\n    \"Validity checks: negative controls and replay as above; interpret permuted-graph RMSE 0.627 (close to DiM level) as evidence that the estimator actually uses graph structure.\"\n  ],\n  \"figure_plan\": [\n    \"Fig. 1 (overview, early placement): conceptual schematic of regression-based GATE estimation: network + random assignment -> exposure features -> fitted outcome regression -> prediction at all-treated and all-control corners (rarely observed exposure states, extrapolation) -> GATE estimate; with the eight-case grid axes (graph family, response shape, spillover range, strength, assignment design) and the two error sources (sampling noise vs exposure-mapping misspecification).\",\n    \"Fig. 2 (main results, two panels): (a) aggregate mean relative RMSE for oracle / frozen estimator / linear / DiM / HT on the final plane (log or broken axis because HT and DiM dwarf the others); (b) per-case relative RMSE of the frozen estimator on the final plane ordered by the factor varied from case 1, annotated with the oracle aggregate and linear aggregate as reference lines.\",\n    \"Fig. 3 (reliability, two panels): (a) strip/dot plot of the 24 per-seed final scores with mean and bootstrap interval; (b) interval coverage 0.839 against the nominal 0.95 line with relative width annotated.\",\n    \"Table 1: factor-contrast table mapping each of the 8 cases to graph, response form, strength, design, with final per-case relative RMSE.\",\n    \"Table 2: search-plane estimator-class comparison (3 variants x 8 cases + mean + bias + coverage) with linear baseline reference, labelled as search plane.\"\n  ],\n@misc{Ugander2013GraphCluster\n@misc{Tiwari2024Quasi\n@misc{Cortez2024TwoStage\n@misc{Jiang2023Mixture\n@misc{Sussman2017Elements\n@misc{Viviano2020Experimental\n@misc{Thiyageswaran2024DataAdaptive\n@misc{Kipf2016GCN\n@misc{Viviano2023CausalClustering\n@misc{Bhattacharya2019Uncertainty\n@misc{Ting2023Limits\n@misc{Chin2018Stein\n@misc{Zhou2020ClusterAdaptive\n@misc{Nandy2019Dense\n@misc{Li2021Noise\n@misc{Alzubaidi2023KNN\n@misc{Karwa2023Admissibility","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false}}
